https://github.com/cblabs-org/u8g2gl ChangeLog

One entry per MINOR for each component, in the form U8G2 uses: the runtime
(the PlatformIO library, runtime-v* tags) and the cli (the npm package u8g2gl,
cli-v* tags). Each PATCH adds a line saying what it added, in the PR that bumps
it. Each runtime line names the U8G2 release it was built and tested against.
The log starts at runtime 0.11 and CLI 0.15 and is not backfilled; earlier
history is in git. See docs/RELEASING.md.


2026-11-24	cli v0.27.xx	U8G2GL
  * 0.27.0: Collections are generated (design_spec 9.2; Q-19 closed).
    ui.hpp declares a typed handle to every named object written in a
    screen, extern <class>& ui_<screen>_<element>, bound in the screen's
    unit to its static object, which stays static; a drawing element and
    an element expanded from a composite's template get none. A <data>
    block's row has a fixed layout from its fields (string max_len + 1,
    int32_t, one-byte bool and enum index, naturally aligned, the row
    padded to 4), and each instance's <data> gets a static buffer of
    capacity x row bytes from its own props, with a constexpr layout,
    handed to the widget at init through attach_rows(). Each <data> on
    each instance also gets a const facade ui_<screen>_<element>_<data>
    whose begin(), typed add_row() (the fields in order, typed as the
    editor's fill thunk passes them; a repeated field as pointer and
    count, with an initializer_list overload) and commit() forward to the
    widget, and whose capacity() is the instance's. The budget reports
    each instance's collection storage exactly, with its derivation, and
    the designer's property panel states that figure for a widget with
    <data>; a project widget without <data> keeps the muted line. New
    diagnostics: U8G2GL_GENERATED_NAME_COLLISION, for a handle, facade or
    storage name that collides with another generated identifier, an
    error naming both; U8G2GL_UNSIZED_DATA, for a <data> whose storage
    cannot be sized (a string with no max_len, no capacity, a count left
    unset), a warning in validate and an error in generate. The scene
    description is version 5 and carries each object's storage, which the
    editor Wasm allocates per scene. Test runs fill each sample set's
    collections through the facades; the objs[i] route and its index guard
    are gone. A composite with a C++ class of its own is still refused,
    its message now naming a later PR. Generated code requires runtime
    0.16.0. Schema 0.7 is unchanged.
2026-11-24	runtime v0.16.xx	U8G2GL
  * 0.16.0: Generated collection storage. New u8g2gl_rows.h: u8g2gl_rows,
    a non-owning, bounds-checked view over a collection's rows in a
    buffer the generator sizes per instance, with typed accessors per
    field and repeat index (int, bool, string truncated at max_len, enum
    index), the capacity in rows, and u8g2gl_row_added() for the generated
    facade. New virtual u8g2gl_obj::attach_rows(data_index, rows), called
    by generated init after the setters and static children; the default
    does nothing. Editor target: u8g2gl_editor_field_type() and
    u8g2gl_editor_rows_fit() check the row layout a host reads from the
    scene. No heap. Schema minor unchanged at 7.
    Built and tested against U8G2 2.36.18.
2026-11-24	cli v0.26.xx	U8G2GL
  * 0.26.0: Sample sets in the designer. A set is samples/<set>.json in
    the project (u8g2gl_samples: 1; values by subject; rows by screen
    file, then element, each row keyed by the manifest's <field> names),
    never compiled: generate and validate ignore samples/, and a project
    with sets generates the same files. Every set is checked against the
    project and each problem is in Problems with the file, the line and
    column, and its JSON path (U8G2GL_SAMPLE_* codes): JSON that does not
    parse, an unknown version, subject, screen, element or field, an
    element with no <data>, a wrong type, a value out of min/max or
    values, a string over max_len (from the instance's props, in
    characters), an array over its repeat, and rows over capacity; what
    checks clean still applies. Sets are project documents, edited and
    undone through the same model as the XML, one undo step per edit.
    The active set is per-browser view state, and the canvas draws its
    values and the drawn screen's rows. The Sample data view is enabled:
    pick, create, duplicate, rename and delete sets (delete asks first);
    a table of each subject's value per set beside the compiled-in
    initial value, inherited where a set leaves it out; every collection
    instance as a typed grid with its capacity, add, remove and reorder;
    paste of a JSON array or CSV with a header row, every refused value
    reported by row and field; selecting a collection on the canvas opens
    its grid. Generated sets (empty, one row, full longest, the last made
    of each font's widest glyph as the new editor-only bridge export
    measureText() measures it) and, while samples/ is empty, the four
    conventional sets: typical, empty, longest-plausible and worst-case.
    The canvas toolbar's Data switcher, cycled by Alt+] and Alt+[. The
    subjects table gains a sample column per set. Test runs check every
    screen with the initial values and with each set, and report parity
    per set and per screen, in the toolbar and the bug report: the stage
    writes a C++ function per set that fills the firmware through the
    ui_set_<subject>() setters and each widget's begin(), access method
    and commit(), reached through ui_screen_<s>.objs[i] and held to the
    generated objs table, and the harness calls it after ui_init(). The
    design server's DELETE /api/file removes a sample set, and only that,
    at the version read. Runtime 0.15.0 and schema 0.7 are unchanged, and
    no generated output moves.
2026-11-22	runtime v0.15.xx	U8G2GL
  * 0.15.0: A style's border draws. Any element with border="true" now
    draws a one-pixel outline it did not draw before, on the device and
    in the designer's preview: set as an attribute, as style_border or
    through a named style, it was stored and never drawn, although the
    schema, design_spec 10 and the designer's help all said it was an
    outline. The framework draws it for every element, in
    u8g2gl_render_object() and u8g2gl_repaint_object(), right after the
    element's render() and inside its clip and state guard: a frame on
    the element's box, in its draw_color, with corners from its radius
    (clamped to the radius bound). Children draw over a container's
    outline. Elements that frame their own box draw no extra outline,
    because the two frames would share edges that cancel under xor: the
    button while its border_width is above 0, and the progress bar and
    the switch always. u8g2gl_obj::draws_own_outline() says so, and only
    those three override it. New public u8g2gl_draw_frame() in
    u8g2gl_draw.h, the button's frame primitive, which the button now
    uses; its pixels are unchanged. Schema minor unchanged at 7.
    Built and tested against U8G2 2.36.18.
2026-11-21	cli v0.25.xx	U8G2GL
  * 0.25.1: Generated code requires runtime 0.15.0, so every generated
    config's two runtime floor lines move; no other generated output
    changes. Any element with border="true" now draws its one-pixel
    outline in the designer's canvas and previews, as on the device. A
    style card's captions name the border each sample draws, a button's
    saying its own border_width frame stands in for the outline when
    border_width is above 0, and the border field's note that no element
    drew it is gone.
  * 0.25.0: Subjects, styles and constants get their own editors, and any
    name renames across files. A pure reference finder in the core
    (core/references/) lists every declaration and use of a subject,
    constant, style, font or image, with the exact text: bind_* and
    <bind_flag_if_*> subject=, typed props and their defaults, <style
    name>, style=, focus_style=, style_font=, a style's font, and each
    #name the expression tokeniser reads (constantTokens(), now exported),
    in every file the project loads; a screen's own style shadows a global
    one there. In the designer, globals.xml's subjects are a table: name,
    type, the value the firmware boots with, max_len with its byte cost,
    and who binds it, each a link that selects the element. A style's card
    previews it on a label and a button drawn by the renderer, each
    captioned with what it draws (no element draws a style's border yet,
    and the border field says so), lists its users and gives the radius
    bound; a constant lists its #name references. Remove is refused for
    every kind while anything the finder reports uses it. Rename... on
    every subject, constant, style, font and image lists each file and the
    generated symbols that change (ui_set_<name>(), ui_subject_, ui_font_,
    ui_fontdata_, ui_image_, ui_style_), refuses an invalid, taken or
    unsafe rename before any write, and applies one undo step per
    document; a name field whose name is referenced asks first: Rename
    everywhere, Only here or Cancel. checkNameRule() is exported from the
    validator. No generated output changes.
2026-11-21	cli v0.24.xx	U8G2GL
  * 0.24.0: Fonts and images reach the editor by injection. The editor
    Wasm compiles in no project font or image: the editor target's
    ui_editor_asset() is emitted empty, and an editor build neither
    compiles nor hashes ui_fonts.cpp or ui_images.cpp, so a font or image
    change never rebuilds it. The bridge gains injectFont(),
    injectImage() and clearInjected(); a font it has nowhere is drawn in
    u8g2_font_4x6_tr with a warning in getLastError(). The design server
    serves GET /api/font/builtin?name=, from the packaged u8g2_fonts.c
    parsed once on the first request. The designer injects every declared
    font and image before each scene, so a widget-free project draws any
    font on the framework Wasm with no toolchain. Widgets get a font or
    image by a type="font"/"image" prop or own a U8G2 one: naming a
    generated ui_font_/ui_fontdata_/ui_image_ symbol is the new error
    U8G2GL_WIDGET_ASSET_SYMBOL, from validate, generate and the editor
    build. The Fonts view is enabled: the project's fonts and every
    built-in, a glyph grid and a string ruler drawn by the renderer, Add
    to project; the property panel's font field gains Browse fonts...
    A <bdf>'s range field gains presets with their compiled sizes,
    including one derived from the project's static text; static text
    outside a <bdf> range is the new warning U8G2GL_GLYPH_OUTSIDE_RANGE.
    The fonts and images sections show sizes and users, images at 1:1,
    and refuse Remove while an item is used. Editor goldens move only in
    the asset lookup; no firmware output changes.
2026-11-17	cli v0.23.xx	U8G2GL
  * 0.23.0: Collection widgets declare their data, and their columns are
    XML. Schema 0.7 (unreleased, so no bump) gains <data> in a widget's
    <api>: the rows its own ingestion API takes, one <field> per argument
    of the access method (int with min/max, bool, string with max_len,
    enum with values; repeat for a string array), with a capacity that is
    an int literal or an int prop. A 0.4-0.6 project that writes one is
    told to declare 0.7. A field's max_len counts usable characters, as a
    string subject's does. New diagnostic: U8G2GL_INVALID_DATA_DECLARATION.
    The <element> children a manifest has always been able to declare are
    now built: a <column> inside a <wd_service_list> is a static child,
    parsed without a name (every loader parses manifests first), checked
    against its <arg>s, called at construction through the declared access
    method after the setters, and listed in the designer's property panel
    as a table with add, remove and reorder, each one undo step. Pass 8 no
    longer refuses them. The editor registry gains add-by-name entries for
    static children and a fill entry per <data>, whose static_assert fails
    the editor build when the fields cannot call the access method; the
    editor bridge gains applyCollectionData(), which the designer does not
    call yet. The scene format is version 4 (an object's static_children).
    Generated firmware changes only for a screen with static children; the
    runtime floor moves to 0.14.0, so every generated config's two floor
    lines move.
2026-11-17	runtime v0.14.xx	U8G2GL
  * 0.14.0: The editor registry fills collection widgets by name. Editor
    target only (U8G2GL_EDITOR), so firmware compiles and pays nothing:
    u8g2gl_editor_class gains an element table, with
    u8g2gl_editor_add_element(), for the static children a manifest's
    <element> declares, and a fill table, with u8g2gl_editor_fill(), for
    each <data>: the field rules, the capacity, and a generated thunk that
    calls the widget's own begin(), access method per row and commit(),
    checking every cell first. u8g2gl_editor_row_call() reads an access
    method's bool return. Schema minor unchanged at 7.
    Built and tested against U8G2 2.36.18.
2026-11-15	cli v0.22.xx	U8G2GL
  * 0.22.0: U8G2's large fonts are named before the linker fails. U8G2
    compiles its 63 fonts over 32 KB only where U8G2_USE_LARGE_FONTS is
    defined (u8g2.h), but declares them everywhere, so a <builtin> naming
    one failed on a platform without the macro only at link time, with a
    bare undefined reference. ui_fonts.cpp now emits, after <U8g2lib.h>,
    an #error for each large <builtin> that names the font and both
    fixes: choose a smaller font, or define U8G2_USE_LARGE_FONTS. Generated
    output changes only for a project that uses a large font. The list is
    extracted from u8g2_fonts.c's #ifdef blocks by the catalogue build
    into u8g2-large-fonts.json, and core gains isLargeFont(); fontFacts()
    gains `large`. The designer's font picker marks each large font
    "large (over 32 KB): needs U8G2_USE_LARGE_FONTS", in its row and in
    the current font's facts, and a "Hide large fonts" filter, off by
    default, leaves them out. The validator does not warn, because it
    cannot see the platform. No schema or runtime change.
2026-11-15	cli v0.21.xx	U8G2GL
  * 0.21.0: Pickers for fonts and asset files in the designer's
    globals.xml form. "+ Built-in font" opens a font picker over U8G2's
    2039 fonts instead of adding an empty <builtin>: a search, filters for
    height, character set and glyph box, and each font's height, decoded
    set and a sample U8G2 itself drew; declared fonts are marked, and
    U8G2's font gallery is linked. A <builtin>'s name keeps it behind
    "Change font...". "+ BDF font", "+ XBM image" and "+ Converted image"
    open a file chooser over the project's .bdf, .xbm or .png files, with
    "Import from your computer..." writing one into fonts/ or images/ and
    never over an existing file; the element gets a name derived from the
    file and, for <convert>, the PNG's width and height. Each src keeps it
    behind "Choose file...". Choosing is one edit and one undo stop;
    Cancel adds nothing. Core gains fontFacts(), which decodes a built-in
    font's box and set from U8G2's naming scheme (19 fonts with
    bare-number suffixes are reported unknown), and
    ProjectFileSystem.writeBytes(). The samples are u8g2-font-samples.json
    (1.25 MB), drawn natively from the pinned U8G2 by npm run
    build:font-samples and loaded only when a picker opens. Generated
    output is unchanged; no schema or runtime change.
2026-11-15	cli v0.20.xx	U8G2GL
  * 0.20.0: u8g2gl.xml is checked against the project vocabulary, the
    table the designer's form is built from. An attribute it does not
    spell on <u8g2gl>, <targets>, <target>, <display> or <output>
    (aliases such as ramBudget are spelled) is now U8G2GL_UNKNOWN_ATTRIBUTE,
    an error at its name with the nearest spelling suggested, where it was
    ignored; an unknown child of <targets>, <target>, <display> or
    <output> is an error at its tag, as under <u8g2gl>. A project that wrote a stray
    attribute and validated before now fails. The removed pins keep their
    own refusal. An unknown attribute keeps the parsed project, so its real
    schema and targets are still checked; when a parse error withholds the
    project, that error is the whole report, with no schema version '' and
    no "No <target> declared" about the placeholder. In the designer,
    Problems shows a fault once: an editor build or test run problem that
    repeats a live one is dropped, and a failed run's problems clear at
    the next edit. Generated output is unchanged; no schema or runtime
    change.
2026-11-14	runtime v0.13.xx	U8G2GL
  * 0.13.0: Implements schema 0.7 (U8G2GL_SCHEMA_VERSION_MINOR 7). Schema
    0.7 adds U8G2's two mirror rotations, which generated code passes to
    the board's constructor as U8G2's own U8G2_MIRROR and
    U8G2_MIRROR_VERTICAL, and removes the pin attributes, which no
    generated file ever carried, so the runtime's behaviour is unchanged.
    Code generated by CLI 0.18 and earlier asserts schema 0.6 and must be
    regenerated, because the schema guard compares the minor exactly while
    the major is 0.
    Built and tested against U8G2 2.36.18.
2026-11-14	cli v0.19.xx	U8G2GL
  * 0.19.0: Schema 0.7. The pins leave u8g2gl.xml: cs, dc, reset and
    address on <display>, <target> or <bus>, and <bus> itself, are
    removed ([D-22]). They never had any effect, because U8G2GL does not
    construct the display; the firmware's board does. Each one written is
    now U8G2GL_REMOVED_ATTRIBUTE, an error at every schema version, at its
    line, saying where the wiring belongs and naming the command below.
    Projects at 0.4, 0.5 and 0.6 that write no pin are still accepted.
    In their place, a constructor snippet: u8g2gl display <setup> prints a
    catalogued display (controller, size, bus, the pins its constructor
    takes, framebuffer), and --snippet [--rotation <r>] [--target <name>]
    prints the board-side code that constructs it, with a FILL_IN_PIN
    placeholder per pin, U8G2GL_TARGET_ROTATION, begin(),
    ui_display_matches() and ui_init(); --json gives both. The designer's
    target section shows the same text, with Copy, where the bus fields
    were. The catalogue records each wiring's pins (bus.pins), read from
    U8G2's U8g2lib.h. rotation gains MIRROR and MIRROR_VERTICAL (schema
    0.7; a 0.4-0.6 project writing one is told to declare 0.7): both are
    layout-neutral, and the contract emits U8G2_MIRROR or
    U8G2_MIRROR_VERTICAL. Generated output changes by its version stamps
    only (schema 0.7, runtime floor 0.13.0); regenerate. Built beside
    runtime 0.13.0.
2026-11-14	cli v0.18.xx	U8G2GL
  * 0.18.0: rotation is honoured. R1 and R3 (90, 270) swap the panel's
    width and height for layout: validation, generation, the scene, the
    designer's canvas, insertion and hit-testing, and a test run all lay
    out and draw a 256x64 panel at R1 as 64x256. R0 and R2 are
    layout-neutral. The panel itself is never rotated, so the §18
    framebuffer line is unchanged. Bounds messages on a turned target say
    "display 64×256 (256×64 rotated 90°) is 64px wide". In the designer a
    rotation that swaps the size goes through the impact preview, as a
    new display does, and applies as one undo step; the picker shows each
    panel at its own size with "shown 64×256 at R1" beside it. ui.hpp now
    carries the display contract (design_spec §16, [D-22]):
    U8G2GL_TARGET_WIDTH and _HEIGHT (after rotation),
    U8G2GL_TARGET_ROTATION (U8G2's own U8G2_Rn) and U8G2GL_TARGET_SETUP
    (diagnostics only), and declares bool ui_display_matches(U8G2&),
    defined in ui.cpp, which now includes <U8g2lib.h>. Generated output
    changes by those lines only; regenerate. On the board side, construct
    with the rotation and check after begin():
        U8G2_SSD1322_NHD_256X64_F_4W_HW_SPI display(U8G2GL_TARGET_ROTATION, DISPLAY_CS, DISPLAY_DC, DISPLAY_RESET);
        display.begin(); if (!ui_display_matches(display)) { /* report it */ }
2026-11-14	cli v0.17.xx	U8G2GL
  * 0.17.0: The designer edits u8g2gl.xml and globals.xml through forms,
    beside the XML. The forms are built from a new declarative vocabulary
    of the two files (every element and attribute, its type, default,
    help and accepted spellings), and tests hold the parsers and the forms
    to it, so every attribute has a control. Each form change is one text
    edit and one undo stop, written where the value is, comments kept; new
    values are written in §5.1's canonical form. Problems show on their
    field, and the Explorer lists each file's sections. In u8g2gl.xml the
    display setup is chosen with a picker (controller, size and variant,
    bus wiring, or search), with a preview of every screen at the new size
    before it is used; bus fields follow the bus family, and a value for
    another family is flagged, not dropped. Targets are added, renamed,
    reordered and removed. The status bar names the active target and
    switches between them: a per-browser view setting, never written to
    the XML. ui_spec §2 gains principle 8, "everything is discoverable",
    and design_spec §5.1 drops the default="true" it never honoured: the
    first target is the default. No change to generated output.
2026-11-10	runtime v0.12.xx	U8G2GL
  * 0.12.0: Implements schema 0.6 (U8G2GL_SCHEMA_VERSION_MINOR 6). Schema
    0.6 adds only an optional icon on <api>, which the designer's Palette
    draws and no generated file carries, so the runtime's behaviour is
    unchanged. Code generated by CLI 0.15 and earlier asserts schema 0.5
    and must be regenerated, because the schema guard compares the minor
    exactly while the major is 0.
    Built and tested against U8G2 2.36.18.
2026-11-10	cli v0.16.xx	U8G2GL
  * 0.16.5: The designer's Explorer lists each screen's elements under it,
    nested as written and in draw order, from the unsaved buffer. A row
    selects its element (opening its screen when another is drawn), and a
    canvas or XML selection lights its row; the tree has the usual keys.
    Each row has an eye that hides the element in the designer only:
    never in the XML, undo, generated code or a test run, which still
    draws the exported view. A hidden element is not drawn, hit or
    outlined; the canvas toolbar shows "N hidden · Show all" and a
    screen's menu offers Show all elements. Rows the sample data hides on
    the device carry a separate mark. The project editor Wasm is rebuilt
    when the CLI's framework sources change. No change to generated
    output.
  * 0.16.4: The designer's property panel puts each property's help in a
    tooltip, as the Palette does for elements, instead of beneath its
    control. An ⓘ after the name opens it on hover, click or keyboard
    focus, as does hovering the name; it shows the help and a facts line with the
    type, an enum's values or a binding's subject types, and the default.
    Escape closes it and leaves the selection alone. Why a field is
    disabled, its problems and the computed-value note stay inline.
    u8g2gl_label's long_mode help is rewritten as user help, and its three
    scroll props now say "Used only when long_mode is scroll or
    scroll_circular." No change to generated output.
  * 0.16.3: The designer's canvas has an Outlines toggle, beside freeze,
    that boxes every drawn element on the overlay, never in the frame, and
    is remembered per browser. A single selected line, hline or vline
    shows a round handle at each end instead of box handles: a line's end
    moves that vertex, and an hline's or vline's end moves along its axis
    with the other end held. A selected line no longer says it cannot
    resize on an axis it has no handle on. No change to generated output.
  * 0.16.2: The designer's first-use fixes. The activity bar draws Lucide
    icons. Saving is visible and stays explicit: the status bar offers
    "● N unsaved · Save", the canvas toolbar has Save for the screen it
    draws, the Explorer marks unsaved files, ⌘S with no editor tab saves
    the canvas's screen and ⌘⌥S saves all. A line's or triangle's handles
    scale its points, and a vertex drags alone; a circle's or ellipse's
    handles change its radii. Circles, ellipses, hlines, vlines and
    pixels can now be selected on the canvas, boxed by what they draw.
    The property panel says why a selected element cannot be resized.
    No change to generated output.
  * 0.16.1: The designer says when its design session ends: a banner
    names the unsaved files and the command for a new session, a failed
    save shows "not saved" in the status bar and brings Output forward,
    and a reload with unsaved changes asks first. A browser opening a
    refused designer link gets a page saying how to start a session,
    instead of JSON; API callers still get JSON. No change to generated
    output.
  * 0.16.0: Schema 0.6: <api icon="..."> names the widget's Palette icon
    from the vendored Lucide set, by name or alias, with a "did you mean"
    for a near miss. The validator accepts 0.4, 0.5 and 0.6; a 0.4 or 0.5
    project using icon is told to declare 0.6. Every framework manifest
    carries its icon, and the Palette reads it from the manifest, falling
    back to the category's icon, then a generic one. Generated code
    requires runtime 0.12.0.
2026-11-03	runtime v0.11.xx	U8G2GL
  * 0.11.1: The runtime version check compares PATCH as well as MINOR:
    U8G2GL_REQUIRE_RUNTIME_AT_LEAST(major, minor, patch), which the
    generator now emits. The two-argument U8G2GL_REQUIRE_RUNTIME_VERSION
    stays, so code generated by CLI 0.15.1 and earlier still compiles.
    The first runtime release tagged under the release scheme.
    Built and tested against U8G2 2.36.18.
2026-11-03	cli v0.15.xx	U8G2GL
  * 0.15.2: Generated code requires runtime 0.11.1 or newer, compared on
    PATCH. The packed CLI reports, and stamps into generated code, its
    release tag's full version (0.15.2-beta.1 on the beta channel). Packing
    needs a cli-v* release tag at HEAD, and a release is published from the
    packed tarball. The first CLI release tagged under the release scheme.
